<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guido Pallavicini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1237">1237</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Guy Pallavicini</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guido Pallavicini was an Italian knight from the Pallavicini family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> who joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>. In 1204 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, granted him Bodonitsa above the pass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>, and he became its first marquis. He built its castle, guarding the road into central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and his family held the marquisate for most of the 13th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Guy came from a distinguished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> family that ruled a series of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> between Parma, Piacenza and Cremona, and his Greek subjects called him Marchesopoulo. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> in 1203, according to William Miller because at home every common man could bring him before the courts. After the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> he went west in autumn 1204 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a> and was made warden of the pass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>. He built his castle at Bodonitsa, modern Mendenitsa, on the ruins of an ancient acropolis, probably that of Pharygai, overlooking the coastal plain of the Malian Gulf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Boniface was killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> in 1207, Guy and his brother, possibly named Rubino, led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> barons who favoured William VI, Marquess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>, against the infant King Demetrius and his mother Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. When the Latin Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-flanders">Henry of Flanders</a> crowned Demetrius in January 1208 and marched south, Guy was among the last to hold out, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmeia</a> of Thebes, rather than submit at the First Parliament of Ravennika. The barons kept their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>, now as imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. Guy gave Zetouni to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> but confiscated property of the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>.</p><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-i-komnenos-doukas">Michael I Komnenos Doukas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and Macedonia, Demetrius left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in winter 1221/22 and Guy became regent (bailli) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>. Only a small vanguard of the promised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> arrived, and after the Nicaean emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Doukas Vatatzes</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> army at the Battle of Poimanenon, the garrison surrendered in December 1224. Theodore released most of the captives, Guy among them. Bodonitsa, now a border march, was saved by its fortifications and by the late crusade, which landed at Halmyros in March 1225 before disease destroyed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Guy made his will on 2 May 1237 and is thought to have died soon after, one of the last living participants in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>. He was succeeded by Ubertino, his son by the Burgundian Sibylla, a cousin of Guy I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Their daughter Mabilia married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azzo-vii-deste">Azzo VII d'Este</a>, Marquess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, and Isabella succeeded her brother but died childless in 1286, after which Thomas Pallavicini seized the castle of Bodonitsa and became marquess.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guy Pallavicini” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1204 Boniface of Montferrat granted the strategic castle of Bodonitsa, above the Thermopylae pass, to the Italian knight <b>Guido Pallavicini</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/marquisate-of-bodonitsa">Marquisate of Bodonitsa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.5,12.5&amp;year=1227" aria-label="Italy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Guido Pallavicini"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5782 3897h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5782 3897h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.5,12.5&amp;year=1227">What was here in 1227? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
